00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:23.000
Agent evaluation is not that straightforward because your agentic application can have different architectures.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:30.000
And you have to build an evaluation pipeline on the basis of architecture.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:39.000
So before discussing the architecture or the evaluation pipeline, I will tell you in general how to do agentic evaluations.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:44.000
So because we are using DPVAL here, I will tell you from the perspective of DPVAL.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:52.000
So DPVAL says that there are three layers which are important to work on any agent.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:58.000
When any agent executes a task or does any work, then three layers work there.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.000
The first layer is the reasoning layer.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:08.000
You can also call this layer a planning layer.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:15.000
DPVAL is called a reasoning layer, but internally it is called planning layer.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.000
The second layer is your action layer.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:25.000
And the third layer is your orchestration layer.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:31.000
Now I will tell you how these three layers work.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:38.000
So your reasoning layer and action layer are sequentially arranged.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:50.000
Meaning that here your reasoning layer is the top layer and below that comes your action layer.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:58.000
Now let's discuss what happens in both the layers.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:08.000
So in the reasoning layer or planning layer, the task of your user comes to you and your reasoning layer generates a detailed plan.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:20.000
Now how will any plan be, it depends on a lot of things.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:24.000
So you have to understand what the work of the reasoning layer actually is.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:25.000
So what is the work of the reasoning layer?

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:30.000
We have said that it generates a detailed plan, but does it generate a straight-forward plan?

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.000
The answer is no.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:42.000
First of all, it understands the user's intent or it understands the task.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:55.000
After that, it breaks down the task into sub-tasks.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:06.000
Any task can have different steps, so it breaks down the big task into small tasks.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:18.000
Then the reasoning layer generates the plan around each of the sub-tasks.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:31.000
It also takes care of the order of execution.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:39.000
So let's say there are two or three steps to do something.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:43.000
Let's say step A, step B, step C.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:48.000
So first of all, it breaks down any task within these three steps.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:56.000
It generates a plan around how to do these three steps and maintains its order.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:02.000
So if A, B and C are in this order, then the plan is also generated in this order.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:06.000
It's not like first B, then A, then C.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:12.000
So the work of the reasoning layer is not to make a detailed plan.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:15.000
It's to understand the user's intent.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:22.000
It breaks down any task into sub-tasks, then generates a plan around each of the sub-tasks.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:26.000
It also takes care of the order of execution.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:30.000
So these are the things that the reasoning layer handles.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:37.000
After that, as soon as this plan is generated, it goes to your action layer.

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:39.000
Now what is the action layer?

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:50.000
Action layer is basically the layer which does or executes the task.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:52.000
It's the layer which does some work.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:56.000
Now it does work, so you should click on it in your mind.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:02.000
This action layer will have tools within this action layer.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:06.000
So this is basically the tooling layer of the agent.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:09.000
So you have a plan generated, it goes to your action layer.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:14.000
Now your action layer will take some actions or do some task with that plan.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:17.000
And your agent will need tools to do those tasks.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:21.000
Now how the action layer works is not straightforward.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.000
It has many layers.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:33.000
First of all, your action layer decides which tool to use.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:35.000
Your agent has many tools.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:41.000
For example, for task A, which tool to use?

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:45.000
This action layer decides through the LLM.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:57.000
After that, second is that the action layer calls the tool with right arguments.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:59.000
You have selected the tool.

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:02.000
Now how to call the tool and what input to give it?

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:04.000
This is also decided by your action layer.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:12.000
Third, how to process the tool result.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:17.000
If your tool gives a result, how to process it?

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:20.000
Suppose your tool did not work and there was an error.

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:22.000
How to handle it?

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:25.000
All this is done by your action layer.

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:39.000
And after that, fourth decides whether the task was executed or retry required.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:53.000
This is your action layer which decides on these steps.

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:55.000
I hope this point is clear to you.

00:09:55.000 --> 00:10:01.000
Reasoning layer does the plan and action layer does the task.

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:04.000
And finally comes your orchestration layer.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:09.000
When you combine these two layers, first reasoning and then action.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:12.000
This combined layer is called the orchestration layer.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:17.000
What is the work of the orchestration layer?

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:23.000
The work of the orchestration layer is that it takes the task from the user.

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:31.000
It takes a task from the user and checks the response generated on it.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:36.000
Basically, it is an end to end layer.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:43.000
And in this orchestration, both planning and action are integrated.

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:46.000
I hope this point is clear to you.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:48.000
First is the planning layer which is making the plan.

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:50.000
Then there is the action layer which is doing the task.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:54.000
And when we combine both together, then comes our orchestration layer.

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:59.000
So this is the work of the agent around these three layers.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:05.000
Now the good thing is that in deep eval, you have metrics around these three layers.

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:15.000
If you remember rag triad, then rag triad was available to judge the different components of your rag pipeline.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:21.000
Similarly, metrics are available around the three layers of your agent in deep eval.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:26.000
I will tell you what are the metrics and then we will discuss about them later.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:33.000
So the planning layer where you are working on planning or reasoning layer.

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:39.000
I say planning layer again and again because it is used to generate plans.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:41.000
You can call it any of the two.

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:46.000
So in your planning or reasoning layer, there are two metrics available in deep eval.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:49.000
First is your plan quality metric.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:52.000
From the name itself, we can understand what this metric does.

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:54.000
It tells you the quality of your plan.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:57.000
The plan generated is how good or bad it is.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:00.000
This metric judges and tells you.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:04.000
The second is your plan adherence metric.

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:07.000
Adherence metric.

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:13.000
The plan that the reasoning layer has generated, how much your agent is able to stick to that plan.

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:15.000
This metric tells you.

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:20.000
Then if I talk about the action layer, then in the action layer where the tool is calling,

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:22.000
Here you have two metrics.

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:28.000
The first is your tool correctness metric.

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:33.000
It tells you that the agent chose the right tool for a given task.

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:36.000
And the second is the argument correctness metric.

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:44.000
From the name itself, this metric tells you that did you pass the right tool or not.

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:48.000
Then if we talk about the orchestration layer.

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:53.000
Where your planning and action layer are end to end combined.

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:55.000
In this, your two metrics come.

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:58.000
The first comes task completion metric.

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:04.000
In the task completion metric, you judge that the given task that the user gave you,

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:06.000
whether it was completed properly or not.

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:10.000
Now you also understand that the task will be completed only when the planning is done well.

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:12.000
And then the execution is done well.

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:16.000
If there is any problem in any layer, whether in the reasoning layer or the action layer,

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:18.000
then the task will not be completed.

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:21.000
So this task completion metric judges this.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:23.000
That's why this is the part of the orchestration layer.

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:27.000
And the second is your step efficiency metric.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:32.000
What does this step efficiency metric tell you?

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:35.000
It tells you that whatever planning layer has generated the plan,

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:39.000
whether your action layer has performed it efficiently or not.

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:43.000
Whether it is that he has taken some extra steps that are not in the plan.

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:46.000
Or he has gone in some other direction.

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:49.000
Or he also judges that the planning layer has generated the plan.

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:53.000
Whether he was taking too many steps that were not required.

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:57.000
So this type of thing is calculated by this step efficiency metric.

00:13:57.000 --> 00:14:03.000
So these are six built-in metrics that DeepEval gives you for your agentic evaluations.

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:05.000
Now what can you do?

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:09.000
Apart from these six metrics, you can use GEval, which we have already read in the rack.

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:14.000
And through GEval you can also create your custom metrics here.

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:17.000
So this is also possible.

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:21.000
Okay, so I have told you about this metric.

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:23.000
Now let's do one thing.

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:29.000
Let's discuss a little more about what type of metrics are there in DeepEval.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:32.000
We have found out about all these built-in metrics.

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:36.000
But they also have a broad categorization.

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:44.000
So there are three types of metrics for your agentic evaluation.

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:53.000
Now the categorization of these three types of metrics is made so that you can tell how these metrics work.

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:58.000
The methodology of these metrics is around the categorization of how they work.

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:01.000
Again, you can ignore this point if you don't know.

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:04.000
But I will repeat these terms many times in the future.

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:07.000
So I am telling you here first.

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:08.000
So there are three types.

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:11.000
The first is end-to-end.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:15.000
Here it is also called black box.

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:18.000
Now this type is not available in DeepEval.

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:20.000
So I will tell you quickly.

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:22.000
What does end-to-end mean?

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:30.000
This metric calculates the relationship between input and output and tells how our application is performing.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:33.000
Now the relationship between input and output,

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:36.000
or checking if the input was this or output was that,

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:37.000
whether it is correct or not,

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:40.000
this type of evaluation is not available in DeepEval.

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:42.000
So this end-to-end, which is category number one,

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:44.000
DeepEval does not support this.

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:46.000
Because it is a very weak category.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:54.000
The second category that DeepEval supports a lot is trajectory-based.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:16:02.000
What happens in trajectory-based evals is that you have the entire trajectory of your agent execution.

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:21.000
Which means what you do here is that you send all the steps or all the trajectories of your agent evals to your eval.

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:28.000
In simple words, if I tell you, then you send the entire trace of your agent to your eval.

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:33.000
So trajectory-based metrics are nothing but your trace-based metrics.

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:38.000
What we do is that we take your entire trace.

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:41.000
We have already seen the trace in the rag application.

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:42.000
What is the trace?

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:44.000
The entire journey of input and output.

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:47.000
So what was planned in planning, what was executed in the executer node,

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:49.000
what tool call happened, what results came.

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:55.000
So all this eval and metrics take all the information and give their answers on the basis of it.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:59.000
Now what is generally the criteria in the trace-based metrics?

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:03.000
First of all, you generate the trace.

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:05.000
I know you will not understand it so well now.

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:07.000
If you see it practically, you will understand it better.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:09.000
Just understand the steps now.

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:11.000
What do you do first?

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:13.000
You generate a trace for any task.

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:15.000
Let's say it was a task.

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:16.000
Task A.

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:20.000
So as soon as I sent this task to the agent, the agent will do its work.

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:27.000
And as soon as the agent finishes his work, what we will do is we will track this entire workflow.

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:30.000
And we will save its trace.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:32.000
Basically, this task went to my agent.

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:34.000
What steps did he do internally and what was his result?

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:37.000
We will lock it in one place through the trace.

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:40.000
After that, what you do is you take this trace.

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:46.000
And you extract information out of it.

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:50.000
Each metric needs different information from the trace.

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:53.000
So you take this information out of these traces.

00:17:53.000 --> 00:18:01.000
After that, the extracted information is given to LLM.

00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:05.000
And here LLM is your LLM as a judge.

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:07.000
As a judge.

00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:09.000
You gave this information.

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:14.000
And on the basis of this information, your LLM gives you a score.

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:17.000
And also gives you a reason why it gave that score.

00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:19.000
Very similar to your rag application.

00:18:19.000 --> 00:18:21.000
You were doing the same in the rag application.

00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:22.000
What did you do?

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:24.000
You used to make a LLM test case.

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:26.000
LLM test case.

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:28.000
What did you do in that?

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:33.000
Let's say if you want to see contextual relevancy, then you had context.

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:37.000
You had to check if it is noisy or not.

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:39.000
You used to extract claims from context.

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:42.000
Then you used to see which of the claims is noisy.

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:46.000
You also had an expected output in some cases.

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:50.000
You used to extract the claim from the reference based metrics.

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:53.000
So exactly the things are there.

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:59.000
What you are doing here is you are breaking down the available information into facts.

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:01.000
Here you are extracting information from the trace.

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:03.000
Whatever you require.

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:06.000
And you are giving that extracted information to LLM as a judge.

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:11.000
And LLM as a judge is giving you back your answer.

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:13.000
I hope this thing is clear to you.

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:17.000
And the third category that comes here.

00:19:17.000 --> 00:19:22.000
That is your component wise evaluation.

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:29.000
In this generally you evaluate your action layer.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:33.000
In the whole trace, there is your reasoning layer.

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:35.000
There is also an action layer and orchestration layer.

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:39.000
Component wise evaluation, you only focus on your action layer.

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:45.000
Means you only focus on that part of the trace where the tool calls are.

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:49.000
You extract the information from the tool calls.

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:53.000
And again you give this extracted information to your LLM as a judge.

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:55.000
He gives you a score.

00:19:55.000 --> 00:19:57.000
So the way to work is exactly the same.

